###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AcquisitionContext
ontoksd:AcquisitionContext rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Context ;
                           rdfs:label "Acquisition Context"@en ;
                           skos:definition """A specific contextual setting in which an instructional entity (e.g., course, learning task, training module) is delivered and within which competence acquisition can be observed, facilitated, or evaluated.
In OntoKSD, AcquisitionContext represents the instructional and environmental conditions under which learners engage in activities designed to develop specific competencies. It combines situational factors (e.g., learning environment, resources, constraints) with pedagogical arrangements (e.g., teaching methods, collaborative structures) that influence the learning process and the demonstration of competence.

Unlike the broader Context, which may describe any situation in which a competence is applied or demonstrated, AcquisitionContext is specifically tied to learning and assessment processes. It situates instructional entities in time and space, providing the background against which evidence of competence acquisition can be generated.

Examples:

A laboratory session in a computer science course where students collaboratively design and test algorithms.

An online simulation platform used for training project management skills under time-constrained scenarios.

A capstone project in an engineering program involving interdisciplinary teamwork and industry mentorship.

Ontologically, AcquisitionContext can be modeled as a specialized form of Context focused on the instructional phase of competence development, typically associated with agents (learners, instructors), tasks, and specific temporal boundaries."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationConstraint
ontoksd:ActivationConstraint rdf:type owl:Class ;
                             owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                                   owl:oneOf ( ontoksd:acMandatory
                                                               ontoksd:acOptional
                                                               ontoksd:conditional
                                                             )
                                                 ] ;
                             rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                             rdfs:comment """ActivationConstraint captures the conditions, limitations, or requirements that delimit how a competence is expected to be exercised within a specific instructional context, including constraints related to tools, representations, time, collaboration, or evaluation conditions.

Ontological Note:

ActivationConstraint constrains the context of competence enactment without altering the conceptual definition of the competence itself.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationMode
ontoksd:ActivationMode rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                             owl:oneOf ( ontoksd:amAnalytical
                                                         ontoksd:amArtifactOriented
                                                         ontoksd:amConstructive
                                                         ontoksd:amInterpretative
                                                         ontoksd:amJustificatory
                                                       )
                                           ] ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """ActivationMode represents the manner in which a competence is enacted within a specific instructional context. It characterizes the cognitive or operational mode through which the competence is mobilized (e.g., constructive, analytical, justificatory), as determined by task design and expected learner actions.

ActivationMode is not necessarily exclusive; a competence may be activated through multiple modes within the same instructional entity.

Ontological Note:

ActivationMode does not redefine the competence itself; it qualifies how the competence is exercised in a particular competence–instructional entity relation.""" ;
                       skos:example """Example Set 1 — Same Competence, Different ActivationModes
Competence: C01 — Develop problem solutions using Automata


1. ActivationMode: Constructive

Instructional context: Learners are required to design and implement a finite automaton from a problem description.

Observable actions:

- Define states and transitions

- Construct the automaton formally

- Ensure acceptance of the target language

Why this is a different mode: The competence is activated through creation and synthesis of an artifact.


2. ActivationMode: Analytical

Instructional context: Learners receive a pre-defined automaton and must analyze its behavior.

Observable actions:

- Trace execution paths

- Identify incorrect transitions

- Explain acceptance or rejection of strings

Key point: The learner does not build the automaton; they reason about an existing one.

➡️ Same competence, different activation mode.


3. ActivationMode: Diagnostic (or Debugging)

Instructional context: Learners are given a faulty automaton and must locate and correct errors.

Observable actions:

- Detect inconsistencies

- Propose minimal fixes

- Justify corrections

Why it matters: This mode emphasizes problem diagnosis, not construction.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 2 — ActivationMode vs. Bloom Level (Important Distinction)
Competence: C08 — Identify Turing Machine variants

1. ActivationMode: Descriptive

Task: “List and describe different variants of Turing Machines.”

- Bloom level: Understand

- Mode: descriptive recognition


2. ActivationMode: Evaluative

Task: “Assess which Turing Machine variant is most appropriate for a given system.”

- Bloom level: Analyze

- Mode: comparative evaluation under constraints


Insight: Bloom’s level changes, but ActivationMode captures the nature of engagement, not the taxonomy itself.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 3 — Specialization Triggered by ActivationMode Change
C07 vs. C07.01 (from CSP Phase 3)

C07 — Develop solutions using Turing Machines

- Typical ActivationMode: Constructive

- Context: single-agent, isolated model


C07.01 — Develop integrated multi-agent solutions using Turing Machines

ActivationModes:

- Constructive (building each TM)

- Integrative (coordinating multiple TMs)

- Analytical (validating system-wide behavior)

Why specialization was needed: The set of activation modes expanded and became structurally different, signaling contextual scope expansion, not just reuse.""" ,
                                    """Example Set 4 — Same Task, Multiple ActivationModes
Task: “The Farmer Robot and the Feeder Robot”

A single task may activate the same competence in multiple modes simultaneously.

Competence: C09 — Apply Turing Machine variants
ActivationMode   x   Manifestation
Applied	      x   Selecting a TM variant for a robot
Analytical	      x   Justifying the choice under system constraints
Coordinative	      x    Aligning variants across agents

ActivationMode is not mutually exclusive: It reflects how the competence is mobilized, not a single step.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationQualifier
ontoksd:ActivationQualifier rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """Definition:

ActivationQualifier represents a contextual descriptor that qualifies how a competence is enacted within a specific instructional entity.
It groups the main categories of contextual qualification used in OntoKSD—such as constraints, modes, and roles—to express how, why, and under which conditions a competence is expected to be exercised in a given instructional context.

Ontological Note:

ActivationQualifier constrains or characterizes the enactment of a competence in context without modifying the conceptual definition of the competence itself.
It is modeled as an abstract superclass to ensure consistent and extensible representation of contextual competence activation metadata.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ActivationRole
ontoksd:ActivationRole rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       owl:equivalentClass [ rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                             owl:oneOf ( ontoksd:arCore
                                                         ontoksd:arExtension
                                                         ontoksd:arSupporting
                                                         ontoksd:arTransversal
                                                       )
                                           ] ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ActivationQualifier ;
                       rdfs:comment """ActivationRole specifies the instructional function assumed by a competence within a given instructional entity, such as primary learning objective, supporting competence, or implicit prerequisite.

ActivationRole describes why a competence is mobilized in a given instructional entity, i.e., the instructional function it plays in that context.

Ontological Note:

The same competence may assume different activation roles across instructional entities; therefore, ActivationRole is modeled as a contextual attribute of competence activation rather than an intrinsic property of the competence.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Artifact
ontoksd:Artifact rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Kind> ;
                 rdfs:comment "A DigitalArtifact is a concrete digital item (e.g., PDF, HTML page, dataset, code repository, video) that embodies or delivers instructional content or assessment materials. It is referenced by instructional entities and courses for access, distribution, preservation, and provenance."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessment
ontoksd:Assessment rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalEntity ;
                   rdfs:comment """Assessment is an InstructionalEntity that specifies an evaluative plan, including purpose, scope, items/tasks, scoring criteria (rubrics), weighting, expected evidence, and conditions of administration. 

It represents the design/specification of an evaluation (the ‘what’ and ‘how’), independent of any particular session or submission.

One Assessment can be realized by multiple AssessmentOccurrence  events across cohorts, dates, or modalities.”"""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentOcurrence
ontoksd:AssessmentOcurrence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Event> ;
                            rdfs:comment """AssessmentOccurrence  is the concrete realization of an Assessment plan: a time-bounded session/submission event conducted within a specific acquisition context (e.g., course offering, section, cohort) with identified participants. gUFO stereotype: Event.

An AssessmentOccurrence (i) produces Evidence (e.g., submissions, rubric ratings, scores), (ii) is linked to an AcquisitionContext, and (iii) involves participant LearningRoles (e.g., Learner, Assessor, Proctor) borne by persons and grounded via an AssessmentRelator that mediates each Person–Occurrence participation. Multiple occurrences may realize the same Assessment plan.”""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentRelator
ontoksd:AssessmentRelator rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentType
ontoksd:AssessmentType rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Assessor
ontoksd:Assessor rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Attitude
ontoksd:Attitude rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Disposition ;
                 rdfs:comment """A specific, learned predisposition to respond consistently in a favorable or unfavorable manner toward a person, object, idea, or situation.

In OntoKSD, Attitude is modeled as a subtype of Disposition, focusing on evaluative orientations that affect behavior and decision-making.

Examples include openness to feedback, ethical commitment, and proactivity. Attitudes influence how competencies are demonstrated and sustained in practice."""@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Attitude"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AttitudeType
ontoksd:AttitudeType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:DispositionType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:ExternalCompetence ;
                       rdfs:comment """Represents a competence defined in the Base Nacional Comum Curricular (BNCC), the official Brazilian national curriculum framework.

BNCCCompetence models competencies at a normative and policy-oriented level, expressing broad educational expectations established by national regulation. These competencies are not operationalized within OntoKSD, but may be aligned with one or more OntoKSD competencies to demonstrate curricular coherence, regulatory compliance, or pedagogical coverage.

BNCCCompetence provides a semantic anchor that enables OntoKSD to relate instructional-level competencies to national curriculum standards, without asserting semantic equivalence or direct substitution.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCGeneralCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCGeneralCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                              owl:disjointWith ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence ;
                              rdfs:comment """Represents a General Competence defined by the BNCC, which expresses broad, transversal educational objectives applicable across multiple knowledge domains, disciplines, and educational stages.

BNCCGeneralCompetence captures high-level dispositions, cognitive attitudes, and overarching capabilities (e.g., critical thinking, communication, ethical responsibility) that are intentionally cross-cutting and non-disciplinary in nature.

Within OntoKSD, BNCCGeneralCompetence instances are used exclusively as external alignment references, allowing multiple domain-specific competencies to be mapped to shared national educational goals, without imposing additional structural or cognitive constraints on the internal competency model.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#BNCCSpecificCompetence
ontoksd:BNCCSpecificCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                               rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:BNCCCompetence ;
                               rdfs:comment """Represents a Specific Competence defined by the BNCC, typically associated with a particular area of knowledge, discipline, or curricular component.

BNCCSpecificCompetence expresses domain-oriented educational expectations at a normative level, serving as a reference point for curriculum design and evaluation. These competencies are more contextualized than general competencies, but still remain non-operational within OntoKSD.

In OntoKSD, BNCCSpecificCompetence is used to establish semantic alignment between externally defined curricular requirements and internally specified competencies, supporting traceability between instructional outcomes and national curriculum mandates.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Certificate
ontoksd:Certificate rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Document ;
                    owl:disjointWith ontoksd:Validation ;
                    rdfs:comment "A document confirming the successful completion of an educational or competence process."@en ;
                    rdfs:label "Certificate"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Competence
ontoksd:Competence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                     owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasCompetenceElement ;
                                     owl:minQualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                     owl:onClass ontoksd:CompetenceElement
                                   ] ;
                   rdfs:comment """A formal, ontological description of an integrated set of knowledge, skills, and dispositions that enables an individual to perform a task or fulfill a role effectively in a given domain.

In OntoKSD, a Competence is a static, reusable conceptual entity that defines what is expected for successful performance, independently of any specific individual or assessment event. It serves as a reference model for competency-based curriculum design, instructional alignment, and performance evaluation."""@en ;
                   rdfs:label "Competence"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceElement
ontoksd:CompetenceElement rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          owl:disjointWith ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill ;
                          <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/description> "Competence elements correspond to personal characteristics that constitute the underlying structure of a competence, as described by Miranda et al. (2018)."@en ;
                          dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Cited as Competency in Miranda, S. et al. (2018). An Ontology-Based Model for Competence Management. Journal of Intelligent Manufacturing."@en ;
                          rdfs:comment """A core constituent of a Competence, representing a single conceptual component that contributes to the overall ability to perform a task.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceElement is an abstract class specialized into three mutually exclusive categories:

Knowledge: Conceptual and factual information relevant to the competence domain.

Skill: The ability to apply knowledge to perform actions or solve problems.

Disposition: Attitudes, values, and behavioral tendencies that influence the performance of the competence.

CompetenceElements are static entities and form the structural building blocks for defining Competences."""@en ;
                          rdfs:label "CompetenceElement"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceRelator
ontoksd:CompetenceRelator rdf:type owl:Class ;
                          rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ,
                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                            owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesCompetence ;
                                            owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                            owl:onClass ontoksd:Competence
                                          ] ,
                                          [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                            owl:onProperty ontoksd:mediatesInstructionalEntity ;
                                            owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                            owl:onClass ontoksd:InstructionalEntity
                                          ] ;
                          owl:hasKey ( ontoksd:hasCompetenceRelatorType
                                     ) ,
                                     ( ontoksd:mediatesCompetence
                                     ) ,
                                     ( ontoksd:mediatesInstructionalEntity
                                     ) ;
                          rdfs:comment "Reifies the relationship between a instructional entity and a competence, with support for annotations."@en ;
                          rdfs:label "CompetenceActivation" ;
                          skos:definition "A reified relation that associates an instructional entity with a competence, specifying whether it is targeted or required as a prerequisite."@en ;
                          skos:scopeNote "Allows the same instructional entity to be linked to multiple competencies with distinct roles (e.g., prerequisite vs. learning outcome)."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceRequirementActivation
ontoksd:CompetenceRequirementActivation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                        rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceRelator .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceTargetedActivation
ontoksd:CompetenceTargetedActivation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceRelator .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#CompetenceType
ontoksd:CompetenceType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:comment "Categoria funcional ou pedagógica da competência (e.g., cognitive, technical, interpersonal)" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Context
ontoksd:Context rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Situation> ;
                rdfs:comment """A situational setting in which a competence or a competence element is demonstrated, applied, or developed.

In OntoKSD, Context captures the environmental, situational, and task-specific conditions that frame the performance of a competence. It may refer to a professional domain, an instructional scenario (e.g., a Problem-Based Learning task), or a typical situation where the competence is relevant.

A context may involve multiple entities such as tasks, agents (e.g., learners, instructors), and competencies, and it exists as a temporary and situational configuration rather than as a permanent, standalone object.
Ontologically, a Context is not treated as a mediating object (relator) with its own relational ontological commitment, but rather as a descriptive construct representing the situational circumstances of competence demonstration."""@en ;
                rdfs:label "Context"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Course
ontoksd:Course rdf:type owl:Class ;
               rdfs:comment "A Course is a curricular unit that aggregates instructional entities (resources, activities, assessments) over a defined time frame and acquisition context, with explicit competency alignment."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Disposition
ontoksd:Disposition rdf:type owl:Class ;
                    rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceElement ;
                    rdfs:comment """A relatively stable tendency or inclination to act or respond in certain ways in specific contexts, reflecting values, motivations, and personal attributes that influence the application of knowledge and skills.

In OntoKSD, Disposition captures the affective and behavioral dimension of competence, which shapes how an individual approaches tasks and interacts with others.

Dispositions are not merely internal attitudes but have observable manifestations that can be linked to professional and social effectiveness."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#DispositionType
ontoksd:DispositionType rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Document
ontoksd:Document rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:comment "A record that may certify competences, competencies, or education."@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Document"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#DocumentDetail
ontoksd:DocumentDetail rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EducationalResource
ontoksd:EducationalResource rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalEntity ;
                            rdfs:comment "An EducationalResource is an instructional entity primarily intended for content delivery and study (as opposed to evaluation). It can be reused across courses and contexts."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Evidence
ontoksd:Evidence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:subClassOf [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                   owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasEvidenceType ;
                                   owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                   owl:onClass ontoksd:Artifact
                                 ] ;
                 rdfs:comment """A verifiable and contextualized record that demonstrates the possession or application of a specific competence or competence element by an individual in a given context.

In OntoKSD, Evidence serves as the bridge between competence descriptions and real-world performance, capturing measurable manifestations of knowledge, skills, and dispositions.

Each piece of evidence is temporally bound (associated with a date or period), may be linked to a specific EvidenceType, and is collected in connection with an instructional activity, professional task, or other relevant context.

Evidence can originate from both formal and informal settings and is essential for supporting assessment, certification, and learning analytics."""@en ;
                 rdfs:label "Evidence"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidenceType
ontoksd:EvidenceType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment """EvidenceType represents a semantic classification of evidence according to the nature of the activity that produced it or the method through which it was collected. It distinguishes among different forms of observable proof used to substantiate the activation and assessment of a competence within a specific instructional context.

In OntoKSD, EvidenceType supports semantic differentiation between heterogeneous forms of evidence, enabling more precise competency evaluation, comparison, and automated reasoning across instructional entities and assessment practices.

Ontological Note:

EvidenceType characterizes the form of evidence without asserting its validity, quality, or sufficiency; such evaluative judgments are handled by assessment processes rather than by the evidence classification itself.

Typical Instances of EvidenceType

SelfAssessment - Evidence generated through an individual’s reflective evaluation of their own competence.

PeerAssessment - Evidence derived from evaluation or feedback provided by peers or colleagues.

TeacherAssessment - Evidence produced through assessment by an instructor, mentor, or formally designated evaluator.

ProjectArtifact - Tangible or digital artifacts generated as deliverables of a task or project, such as reports, models, source code, or designs.

PerformanceObservation - Evidence obtained through direct observation of task execution in real, simulated, or controlled environments.

WrittenExam - Evidence based on performance in written assessments designed to evaluate specific knowledge, skills, or competencies.

Presentation - Evidence consisting of oral, visual, or multimedia presentations used to demonstrate competence to an audience.

PortfolioEvidence - A curated collection of artifacts or records demonstrating sustained or progressive competence over time."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetence
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """A contextualized, individual-specific record that links a Competence to concrete proof of its acquisition or demonstration.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceDetail captures measurable data derived from assessment activities, performance records, or verifiable artifacts.
It includes contextual metadata such as acquisition date, assessment method, and environment in which the competence was demonstrated.

Unlike Competence, which is a static reference model, CompetenceDetail is dynamic and time-dependent, reflecting the actual performance of an individual at a given moment.

An evidential record of a person’s demonstrated competence in a context; bearer of ProficiencyLevel qualities.\"@en ."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#EvidencedCompetenceElement
ontoksd:EvidencedCompetenceElement rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                   rdfs:comment """An observed, measurable instantiation of a CompetenceElement (Knowledge, Skill, or Disposition) demonstrated by a specific individual.

In OntoKSD, CompetenceElementDetail specifies how a particular element of a competence has been evidenced, including the performance indicators, measurement scales, and contextual factors used for assessment.

This class enables fine-grained tracking of individual capabilities over time, supporting both formative and summative evaluation practices."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ExternalCompetence
ontoksd:ExternalCompetence rdf:type owl:Class ;
                           rdfs:comment """Represents a competence defined in an external reference framework, normative document, or institutional guideline, which is not authored within the OntoKSD competency specification process.

ExternalCompetence is used to reference, contextualize, and align OntoKSD competencies with competencies defined by authoritative external sources (e.g., national curricula, international frameworks, or professional standards), while preserving the conceptual autonomy of the internal competency model.

Instances of ExternalCompetence do not define learning outcomes directly within OntoKSD. Instead, they serve as alignment targets, supporting curriculum mapping, compliance analysis, and interoperability with external educational frameworks.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#FormativeCredit
ontoksd:FormativeCredit rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#InstructionalEntity
ontoksd:InstructionalEntity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                            rdfs:comment """An InstructionalEntity is an abstract educational artifact—such as a course component, learning activity, resource, or assessment—designed to enable the acquisition, reinforcement, or evaluation of competencies in a specific context.

InstructionalEntity encompasses any deliberately created learning experience or educational object, regardless of format, that is linked to targeted competencies either as learning goals or prerequisite requirements. It also serves as a source of competence evidence, as the performance of learners within the entity’s scope can be evaluated to determine the extent of competence acquisition.

Definition:
An InstructionalEntity is any structured educational activity, resource, or assessment designed with the explicit purpose of supporting, validating, or evidencing one or more competencies in a specific instructional and contextual setting.

Examples include:
– Formal learning units: courses, modules, workshops, seminars.
– Practical or performance-based activities: projects, simulations, problem-based learning scenarios, internships.
– Assessments and evaluation tools: written exams, presentations, portfolios, skill demonstrations.
– Digital learning resources: e-learning modules, interactive simulations, multimedia learning objects.

Ontological notes:

InstructionalEntity is an abstract class that can be specialized according to different educational domains, such as formal education, corporate training, or informal learning.

It connects to Competence instances via explicit relations such as targetsCompetence (competencies intended for development) and requiresCompetence (prerequisite competencies).

It is anchored to an AcquisitionContext, which defines the environmental and pedagogical conditions under which it is implemented.

Evidence generated from learner engagement in an InstructionalEntity is represented through Evidence and EvidenceType, supporting competency assessment and learner profiling."""@en ;
                            rdfs:label "Instructional Entity"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Interval
ontoksd:Interval rdf:type owl:Class ;
                 rdfs:comment "A bounded range on a Scale used to define a ProficiencyLevel."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Knowledge
ontoksd:Knowledge rdf:type owl:Class ;
                  rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceElement ;
                  rdfs:comment """A theoretical or practical understanding of facts, concepts, principles, and procedures relevant to a specific domain.

In OntoKSD, Knowledge represents the cognitive dimension of a competence — what an individual knows and can recall, explain, or apply.

It may include both declarative knowledge (factual information) and procedural knowledge (knowing how to perform tasks), and is typically acquired through study, instruction, or experience."""@en ;
                  rdfs:label "Knowledge"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeSkill
ontoksd:KnowledgeSkill rdf:type owl:Class ;
                       rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Relator> ;
                       rdfs:comment "A relator that semantically links a knowledge element and a skill to represent how a specific skill is applied to a specific knowledge domain. It is not itself a CompetenceElement."@en ,
                                    """KnowledgeSkill representa a combinação específica entre um conhecimento e uma habilidade (por exemplo, \"aplicar autômatos finitos\").

Essa combinação relacional pode ser instanciada como um gufo:Relator, pois:
  - Depende de Knowledge e Skill;
  - Pode ter propriedades próprias (ex: verbo de ação, nível de Bloom, evidência).""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#KnowledgeType
ontoksd:KnowledgeType rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Learner
ontoksd:Learner rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Role> ;
                rdfs:comment """A LearningRole is an educational role assumed by a learner within a course, discipline, or instructional activity. It contextualizes the learner’s participation by specifying the responsibilities, expected behaviors, and competencies associated with that role.

Core Idea

It represents how a learner is positioned within a specific instructional context (e.g., student in Algorithms, group leader in a project, teaching assistant in a course).

It provides semantic links between roles and competencies, enabling the specification of which competencies are required, expected, or likely to be developed when acting in a given role.

It allows for the contextual interpretation of performance evaluations, since competence evidence is relative to the role played by the learner at the time."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningActivity
ontoksd:LearningActivity rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:InstructionalEntity ;
                         rdfs:comment "A LearningActivity is an instructional entity that specifies an educational action to be performed by learners (individually or in teams) to develop or demonstrate competencies." .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningNeed
ontoksd:LearningNeed rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:comment "A gap between current and desired competence levels requiring educational intervention."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#LearningTask
ontoksd:LearningTask rdf:type owl:Class ;
                     rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:LearningActivity ;
                     rdfs:comment "A LearningTask is a concrete, often assessable learning activity (e.g., a PBL case task) that can target competencies and produce evidence through performance and deliverables."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#PerformanceClassScheme
ontoksd:PerformanceClassScheme rdf:type owl:Class ;
                               rdfs:subClassOf skos:OrderedCollection .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Person
ontoksd:Person rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyLevel
ontoksd:ProficiencyLevel rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#Quality> ;
                         rdfs:comment """A classification indicating the depth or complexity of a Competence or Competence Element.

(Proficiency Level) is a set of levels for classifying competences in doing actions.

A quality that inheres in Evidence or (Competence/CompetenceElement)Detail indicating the attained proficiency."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ProficiencyValue
ontoksd:ProficiencyValue rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#QualityValue> ;
                         rdfs:comment "A reified value for proficiency (e.g., Beginner/Intermediate/Advanced/Expert or A–D)."@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Profile
ontoksd:Profile rdf:type owl:Class ;
                rdfs:comment """A representation of an individual's competences, learning, education, and roles.

Representa o perfil educacional do aluno: competências evidenciadas, papéis, evidências, lacunas, etc.
É a entidade central da visão educacional — é a partir dela que se derivam análises como “lacuna de competência”, “progresso”, “evidências” etc."""@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ResourceType
ontoksd:ResourceType rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#RoleDetail
ontoksd:RoleDetail rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Scale
ontoksd:Scale rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:comment "A measurement scale (numeric, Likert, etc.) used to interpret observed performance into proficiency levels.\""@en ;
              rdfs:label "Scale"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Skill
ontoksd:Skill rdf:type owl:Class ;
              rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:CompetenceElement ,
                              [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                owl:onProperty ontoksd:hasSkillLevel ;
                                owl:qualifiedCardinality "1"^^xsd:nonNegativeInteger ;
                                owl:onClass <http://purl.org/edu/bloom#SkillLevel>
                              ] ;
              rdfs:comment """The ability to apply knowledge effectively to perform tasks, solve problems, or produce desired outcomes in a given context.

In OntoKSD, Skill captures the functional and operational dimension of competence, encompassing cognitive skills (e.g., reasoning, problem-solving), technical skills (e.g., programming, instrument operation), and interpersonal skills (e.g., teamwork, negotiation).

Skills are observable through performance and are often assessed via demonstrations, simulations, or task completion."""@en ;
              rdfs:label "Skill"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#SkillType
ontoksd:SkillType rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#StructureType
ontoksd:StructureType rdf:type owl:Class ;
                      rdfs:comment "Forma como a competência está estruturalmente organizada (e.g., atomic, pair-based, composite)" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Validation
ontoksd:Validation rdf:type owl:Class ;
                   rdfs:subClassOf ontoksd:Document ;
                   rdfs:comment "A type of document certifying the achievement or recognition of a competence."@en ;
                   rdfs:label "Validation"@en .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd/rcd#RCD
rcd:RCD rdf:type owl:Class .


###  http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept
skos:Concept rdf:type owl:Class .


#################################################################
#    Individuals
#################################################################

###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Abstract
ontoksd:Abstract rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontoksd:StructureType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AcessmentProject
ontoksd:AcessmentProject rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  ontoksd:AssessmentType ;
                         rdfs:label "Acessment Project" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#AssessmentPresentation
ontoksd:AssessmentPresentation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                        ontoksd:AssessmentType ;
                               rdfs:label "Presentation" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Atomic
ontoksd:Atomic rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                        ontoksd:StructureType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Composite
ontoksd:Composite rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           ontoksd:StructureType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#ComputingDomain
ontoksd:ComputingDomain rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 ontoksd:CompetenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Exam
ontoksd:Exam rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                      ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Foundational
ontoksd:Foundational rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontoksd:CompetenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Instructional
ontoksd:Instructional rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                               ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Observed
ontoksd:Observed rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                          ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Peer_Assessment
ontoksd:Peer_Assessment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 ontoksd:EvidenceType ;
                        rdfs:label "Peer Assessment" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Portfolio
ontoksd:Portfolio rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                           ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Presentation
ontoksd:Presentation rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontoksd:EvidenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Professional
ontoksd:Professional rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontoksd:CompetenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Project_Artifact
ontoksd:Project_Artifact rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  ontoksd:EvidenceType ;
                         rdfs:label "Project Artifact" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#SelfReported
ontoksd:SelfReported rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                              ontoksd:EvidenceType ;
                     rdfs:label "Self Reported" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Self_Assessment
ontoksd:Self_Assessment rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                 ontoksd:EvidenceType ;
                        rdfs:label "Self Assessment" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#Transversal
ontoksd:Transversal rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             ontoksd:CompetenceType .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#WrittenExam
ontoksd:WrittenExam rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                             ontoksd:AssessmentType ;
                    rdfs:label "Written Exam" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#acMandatory
ontoksd:acMandatory rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                    rdfs:label "mandatory" ;
                    skos:definition """mandatory denotes an activation constraint in which a specified condition, resource, or requirement must be satisfied for the competence to be considered properly enacted within an instructional entity.

Example:

The use of a finite automata simulator plays a mandatory constraint when learners are required to construct and validate automata exclusively using a designated simulation tool.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#acOptional
ontoksd:acOptional rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                   rdfs:label "optional" ;
                   skos:definition """optional denotes an activation constraint in which a specified condition or resource may be used to support competence enactment but is not required for successful task completion or assessment.

Example:

The use of graphical modeling software plays an optional constraint when learners may choose to employ it to visualize automata, while alternative representations are also accepted.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#amAnalytical
ontoksd:amAnalytical rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                     rdfs:label "analytical" ;
                     skos:definition """Analytical denotes an activation mode in which a competence is enacted through systematic examination, decomposition, or evaluation of structures, behaviors, or properties of a given model, system, or artifact.

Clarification: This mode emphasizes reasoning about existing entities rather than producing new ones.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#amArtifactOriented
ontoksd:amArtifactOriented rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                           rdfs:label "artifact-oriented" ;
                           skos:definition """Artifact-oriented denotes an activation mode in which a competence is enacted through the production, modification, or use of tangible or digital artifacts that serve as observable evidence of performance.

Clarification: The emphasis lies on the artifact as a product of activity and as a vehicle for evaluation, rather than on the internal cognitive processes that led to its creation.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#amConstructive
ontoksd:amConstructive rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                       rdfs:label "constructive" ;
                       skos:definition """Constructive denotes an activation mode in which a competence is enacted through the cognitive process of creating, assembling, or synthesizing a solution or model. The emphasis lies on the act of construction itself, independent of whether the resulting artifact is preserved or assessed.

Clarification: The focus is on cognitive synthesis and design decisions rather than on the material properties of the produced artifact.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#amInterpretative
ontoksd:amInterpretative rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                         rdfs:label "interpretative" ;
                         skos:definition """Interpretative denotes an activation mode in which a competence is enacted through the attribution of meaning to formal representations, models, or artifacts, including explanation, reading, and semantic interpretation.

Clarification: The focus is on understanding and explaining significance rather than evaluating correctness or constructing solutions.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#amJustificatory
ontoksd:amJustificatory rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                        rdfs:label "justificatory" ;
                        skos:definition """Justificatory denotes an activation mode in which a competence is enacted through the articulation and defense of decisions, choices, or solutions using explicit reasoning, criteria, or constraints.

Clarification: This mode emphasizes argumentation and rationale rather than construction or analysis alone.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#arCore
ontoksd:arCore rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
               rdfs:label "core" ;
               skos:definition """core denotes an activation role in which a competence constitutes a central learning objective of an instructional entity and is explicitly targeted by assessment criteria.

Example:

The competence Develop solutions using Turing Machines (C07) plays a core role in an instructional entity whose primary objective is the construction of executable Turing Machine models.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#arExtension
ontoksd:arExtension rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                    rdfs:label "extension" ;
                    skos:definition """extension denotes an activation role in which a competence is mobilized beyond its foundational scope in order to address extended, integrated, or system-level instructional demands.

Example (usage context)

The competence Develop solutions using Turing Machines (C07) plays an extension role when it is enacted in a multi-agent system task that requires coordination of multiple Turing Machines, as formalized through the specialized competence C07.01.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#arSupporting
ontoksd:arSupporting rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                     rdfs:label "supporting" ;
                     skos:definition """supporting denotes an activation role in which a competence is mobilized to enable, facilitate, or enhance the enactment of another competence that plays a core role, without being the main focus of assessment.

Example:

The competence Test automata using simulators (C03) plays a supporting role when it is used to validate automata developed as part of a task whose core competence is Develop problem solutions using Automata (C01).""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#arTransversal
ontoksd:arTransversal rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                      rdfs:label "transversal" ;
                      skos:definition """transversal denotes an activation role in which a competence is mobilized across multiple instructional entities or tasks, often implicitly, without being the primary or explicitly assessed focus in a specific context.

Example:
Basic programming competence plays a transversal role when it is assumed across several computational modeling tasks but is not directly assessed in any single instructional entity.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#conditional
ontoksd:conditional rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ;
                    skos:definition """conditional denotes an activation constraint that applies only under specific conditions or task states, becoming relevant when predefined criteria are met during competence enactment.

Example:

The requirement to justify the choice of a Turing Machine variant plays a conditional constraint when multiple variants are available and alternative design decisions are possible.""" .


###  http://purl.org/ontoksd#evFormalArtifact
ontoksd:evFormalArtifact rdf:type owl:NamedIndividual ,
                                  ontoksd:EvidenceType ;
                         rdfs:comment "FormalArtifact" ;
                         rdfs:label "FormalArtifact" .


